  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe transient ridge over the Western United States will get pushed \neast this evening into Wednesday morning by an upper level low \npressure system. This will bring increased high cloud coverage into \nWednesday, but conditions will remain dry. Temperatures also are not \nexpected to change much, staying within a few degrees of today's \nhigh temperatures. The upper level low is expected to dig south \nThursday, which will allow for ridging to build back over Southern \nCalifornia and for the pressure gradient to turn offshore.  \n\nGuidance continues to show weak to locally moderate Santa Ana winds \non Thursday and Friday. Wind gusts look to peak in the mountains, \nfoothills, and passes at 35 to 45 mph, with gusts to 45 mph confined \nto wind prone locations. The pressure gradient is most offshore on \nThursday, which is when the strongest winds will be. In addition to \nwinds, temperatures will peak Thursday and Friday. High temperatures \nwill be 5 to 15 degrees above average, with the warmest conditions \non Friday. West of the mountains, highs will be in the mid to upper \n70s to mid-80s. \n\nBy Saturday, most guidance has high temperatures cooler than Friday, \nbut still 5 to 12 degrees above average. More noticeable cooling is \nexpected early next week as a more persistent troughing pattern sets \nup along the Western United States. While our first chance at \nprecipitation remains Sunday, latest model trends have pushed the \ntiming back to Sunday night/Monday. ECMWF and GFS ensemble \nmembers continue to show mostly light rain for that time period. \nSnow levels look to stay above 8000 ft. A stronger trough that is \nexpected to dig further south is expected for the middle of next \nweek. This system has the potential to bring more substantial \nprecipitation, increased winds, and continued cooler conditions to\nSouthern California. There remains uncertainty in the details \nsurrounding timing and precipitation amounts. NBM chances for 1 \ninch or more of rainfall Tuesday (Dec 1) and Wednesday (Dec 2) are\n55 to 65 percent for the coast and valley valleys, 75 to 85 \npercent for the mountains. The chance for 2 inches or more of \nrainfall ranges from around 25 to 35 percent for the coast and \nvalleys, to 45 to 55 percent for the San Bernardino County \nmountains. The chance for 5 inches or more of rainfall is around 5\nto 10 percent for the San Bernardino County mountains.",
